 "literal": "So many elephants too, trembling with arrows, / having no action to perform, the armies ceased indeed. / The horses of glorious fame, all, / with valorous warriors, fell there indeed. / The noble warriors who came bringing chariots, all, / as shields covered their faces, perished together indeed. / The drums with hair-covered surfaces, tightly bound and of excellent workmanship, / because of the absence of those who carry them, perished where they remained indeed. / Because long spears pierced their chests smeared with sandalwood paste, / the kings too, having fought, perished in the battlefield now indeed. / What will become indeed of the paddy fields, / where women with braceleted hands, wearing water-lily ornaments, / having eaten fresh flattened rice, plunge into cool water, / of the settlements of unceasing new wealth, / of the beautiful expanse of their wide-spaced country?",
 "translation": "So many elephants, trembling with arrows, have ceased from action, their armies gone. The horses of glorious fame have all fallen there along with brave warriors. The noble men who came on chariots have all perished together, covered by their shields. The drums with hair-covered surfaces, tightly bound and of excellent workmanship, have perished where they lay, as there is no one left to carry them. Long spears having pierced their chests smeared with sandalwood paste, the kings too fought and died on the battlefield. Now, what will become of their wide-spaced country, with its beautiful expanse and unceasing wealth, where women with braceleted hands, wearing water-lily ornaments, eat fresh flattened rice and plunge into the cool waters of the paddy fields?",
 "modern": "அம்புகளால் நடுங்கி, எத்தனையோ யானைகள் இனிச் செய்யத்தக்க செயல் ஏதுமின்றி ஒழிந்தன. வெற்றிப் புகழால் சிறந்த குதிரைகள் எல்லாம் வீரர்களோடு அங்கேயே மடிந்தன. தேர்களில் வந்த சான்றோர்கள் அனைவரும் கேடயங்கள் முகத்தை மறைக்க ஒருசேர அழிந்தனர். நன்றாகக் கட்டப்பட்டுத் தொழில் மாண்புற்ற மயிர்க்கண் முரசுகள், அவற்றைச் சுமப்பவர் இல்லாததால் இருந்த இடத்திலேயே அழிந்தன. சந்தனம் பூசிய மார்பில் நீண்ட வேல்கள் பாய்ந்ததால், வேந்தர்களும் போரிட்டுக் களத்திலேயே மடிந்தனர். இனி, வளையல் அணிந்த கைகளை உடைய மகளிர் ஆம்பல் மலர்களைச் சூடி, பச்சரிசி அவலைத் தின்று குளிர்ந்த நீரில் பாயும் வயல்களைக் கொண்டதும், புதிய வருவாய் நீங்காத ஊர்களைக் கொண்டதுமான, அழகிய பரந்த இடத்தையுடைய அவர்தம் நாடு என்னவாகுமோ?",
 "explanation": "The poet Paraṇar is singing about the tragic aftermath of the battle between the Chola king Vēṟpahṟaḍakkai Peruviṟaṟkiḷḷi and the Chera king Kudakko Nedunceralathan. Both kings fought with immense valour and died together on the battlefield, leaving their armies and kingdoms leaderless. The poet describes the devastation of the war: elephants are wounded, horses and chariot-warriors have fallen, and the royal drums lie abandoned with no one to carry them. He then contrasts this grim scene of death with the peaceful, fertile landscape of their countries, where women used to play in the paddy fields, eat fresh flattened rice, and bathe in cool waters. The poem belongs to the tumpai tiṇai, which deals with battle, and specifically the tuṟai 'tokai nilai', depicting the collective fall of both sides. Through this contrast, the poet laments the futility of war, wondering what will now become of these beautiful, prosperous lands that have lost their rulers.",
